INSERT INTO `questions` VALUES (1,'2026-11-02 06:42:14.154583','EASY','In C, %d or %i outputs a signed decimal integer. %f is for floating-point values, %c is for single characters, and %s is for null-terminated character strings. Misconception: Novices often mistakenly think %d stands for double.',1,'What is the correct format specifier to print a standard signed int variable in C using printf?','MCQ',NULL,1,1,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',0,1,NULL),(2,'2026-11-02 06:42:14.159583','EASY','In C, dividing two integers truncates any fractional portion toward zero. 7 / 2 produces 3, not 3.5. Misconception: Beginners assume the fractional part is preserved or rounded up.',1,'What is the value of \'result\' after executing this C code?\n\nint a = 7;\nint b = 2;\nint result = a / b;','MCQ',NULL,1,2,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',0,1,NULL),(3,'2026-11-02 06:42:14.162583','MEDIUM','Character literals in C are represented by integer ASCII values (\'A\' is 65). Adding 2 yields 67. Because the %d specifier is used, it prints the integer 67 instead of character \'C\'. Misconception: Expecting the letter \'C\' due to ignoring the %d integer specifier.',1,'What is printed by the following C program?\n\n#include <stdio.h>\nint main() {\n    char ch = \'A\';\n    printf(\"%d\", ch + 2);\n    return 0;\n}','MCQ',NULL,1,3,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',0,1,NULL),(4,'2026-11-02 06:42:14.165583','MEDIUM','By definition in the ISO C standard, sizeof(char) is always guaranteed to be exactly 1 byte. Misconception: Students familiar with Java assume char is 2 bytes for 16-bit Unicode.',1,'In standard C (C99 and later), which of the following is guaranteed regarding sizeof(char)?','MCQ',NULL,1,4,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',0,1,NULL),(5,'2026-11-02 06:42:14.168583','HARD','The expression 5 / 2 involves two integer literals, evaluating to integer 2. That integer is then converted to float 2.0f upon assignment. Misconception: Believing that assigning to a float variable forces the right-hand division to execute in floating-point.',1,'What will be printed by the following code on a system with standard IEEE 754 floats?\n\n#include <stdio.h>\nint main() {\n    float f = 5 / 2;\n    printf(\"%.2f\", f);\n    return 0;\n}','MCQ',NULL,1,5,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',0,1,NULL),(6,'2026-11-02 06:42:14.170583','HARD','A variable qualified as const is read-only. The post-increment operator (x++) attempts to modify x, resulting in a compilation error. Misconception: Assuming const variables can be modified via post-increment or that the error is deferred to runtime.',1,'What happens when attempting to compile and execute the following C code?\n\n#include <stdio.h>\nint main() {\n    const int x = 10;\n    int y = x++;\n    printf(\"%d\", y);\n    return 0;\n}','MCQ',NULL,1,6,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',0,1,NULL),(7,'2026-11-02 06:42:14.173583','EASY','Multiplication (*) has higher precedence than addition (+), so b * 2 evaluates first (20), followed by 5 + 20 = 25. Misconception: Evaluating strictly left-to-right yields (5 + 10) * 2 = 30.',1,'What is the value of \'c\' after executing this code snippet?\n\nint a = 5;\nint b = 10;\nint c = a + b * 2;','MCQ',NULL,2,7,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',0,1,NULL),(8,'2026-11-02 06:42:14.176582','EASY','The single equals sign (=) assigns the value on the right to the variable on the left. The double equals sign (==) compares two values for equality, returning 1 (true) or 0 (false). Misconception: Accidental assignment inside if conditions (if (x = 5)) is a notorious beginner bug.',1,'What is the fundamental difference between \'x = 5\' and \'x == 5\' in C?','MCQ',NULL,2,8,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',0,1,NULL),(9,'2026-11-02 06:42:14.179583','MEDIUM','++a is pre-increment: a becomes 6, then assigned to b (b=6). a++ is post-increment: current value of a (6) is assigned to c (c=6), then a increments to 7. Final values: a=7, b=6, c=6. Misconception: Confusing the timing of value evaluation between prefix and postfix increment.',1,'What is the output of the following C program?\n\n#include <stdio.h>\nint main() {\n    int a = 5;\n    int b = ++a;\n    int c = a++;\n    printf(\"%d %d %d\", a, b, c);\n    return 0;\n}','MCQ',NULL,2,9,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',0,1,NULL),(10,'2026-11-02 06:42:14.182582','MEDIUM','In a logical AND (&&) expression, if the left operand is false (0), the remaining operand is never evaluated (short-circuited). Since x is 0, ++y never runs, leaving y as 5. Misconception: Expecting all sub-expressions inside parentheses to always be executed.',1,'What will be printed by this code due to short-circuit evaluation?\n\n#include <stdio.h>\nint main() {\n    int x = 0;\n    int y = 5;\n    if (x && ++y) {\n        y += 10;\n    }\n    printf(\"%d\", y);\n    return 0;\n}','MCQ',NULL,2,10,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',0,1,NULL),(11,'2026-11-02 06:42:14.185583','HARD','The modulus operator returns the remainder of integer division. 17 divided by 5 is 3 with a remainder of 2 (5 * 3 + 2 = 17). Misconception: Mistaking % for percentage calculation or floating-point division.',1,'What does the modulus operator (%) do in C, and what is the result of \'17 % 5\'?','MCQ',NULL,2,11,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',0,1,NULL),(12,'2026-11-02 06:42:14.188584','HARD','The condition (10 > 20) evaluates to false (0), so the expression resolves to the value after the colon, which is b (20). Misconception: Assuming ternary operators return boolean flags rather than evaluating the chosen branch value.',1,'What is the output of the following C snippet utilizing the conditional ternary operator?\n\n#include <stdio.h>\nint main() {\n    int a = 10, b = 20;\n    int max = (a > b) ? a : b;\n    printf(\"%d\", max);\n    return 0;\n}','MCQ',NULL,2,12,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',0,1,NULL),(13,'2026-11-02 06:42:14.191583','EASY','C passes function arguments by value. To allow scanf to write data into the caller\'s variable, it requires a pointer to the variable\'s memory address. Misconception: Believing & is an optional syntax or merely an integer indicator.',1,'Why does scanf require the address-of operator (&) for fundamental variables like \'scanf(\"%d\", &num);\'?','MCQ',NULL,3,13,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',0,1,NULL),(14,'2026-11-02 06:42:14.193583','EASY','%c reads or writes a single character. %s is for null-terminated strings, and %d is for signed integers. Misconception: Using %s to read a single char, which can cause memory corruption because strings expect a null terminator.',1,'What is the format specifier used to read or print a single char in C?','MCQ',NULL,3,14,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',0,1,NULL),(15,'2026-11-02 06:42:14.196582','MEDIUM','The specifier %.3f formats the number to 3 decimal places with standard mathematical rounding. Since the fourth digit is 5, 3.1415... rounds up to 3.142. Misconception: Assuming printf strictly truncates without rounding (expecting 3.141).',1,'What is the output of this printf statement?\n\n#include <stdio.h>\nint main() {\n    double pi = 3.14159265;\n    printf(\"%.3f\", pi);\n    return 0;\n}','MCQ',NULL,3,15,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',0,1,NULL),(16,'2026-11-02 06:42:14.197583','MEDIUM','The %s format specifier in scanf stops reading at the first whitespace character (space, tab, newline). Therefore, str only contains \'Hello\'. fgets() must be used to read entire lines with spaces. Misconception: Assuming %s reads until the Enter key.',1,'When reading a string using \'scanf(\"%s\", str);\' where char str[50];, what happens if the user enters \'Hello World\'?','MCQ',NULL,3,16,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',0,1,NULL),(17,'2026-11-02 06:42:14.200583','HARD','According to the C standard library specification, printf returns the total count of characters successfully written to the output stream. Misconception: Most beginners believe printf has a void return type or always returns 0 or 1.',1,'What value does printf return upon successful execution in C?','MCQ',NULL,3,17,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',0,1,NULL),(18,'2026-11-02 06:42:14.202582','HARD','A number directly preceding the specifier designates the minimum field width. If the printed data is shorter than the width (42 is 2 characters), it is right-aligned and padded with leading spaces (3 spaces + 42). Misconception: Confusing minimum field width (%5d) with decimal precision (%.5d).',1,'Given this C snippet:\n\n#include <stdio.h>\nint main() {\n    int x = 42;\n    printf(\"%5d\", x);\n    return 0;\n}\n\nWhat does the format specifier \'%5d\' specify?','MCQ',NULL,3,18,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',0,1,NULL),(19,'2026-11-02 06:42:14.205583','EASY','In C, integer 0 evaluates to false. ANY non-zero integer (including negative numbers like -1 or -100) evaluates to true. Misconception: Many students believe negative numbers evaluate to false.',1,'In C, which of the following values is evaluated as boolean FALSE in conditional expressions?','MCQ',NULL,4,19,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',0,1,NULL),(20,'2026-11-02 06:42:14.207582','EASY','The statement if (x = 5) performs assignment, not comparison. The assignment expression evaluates to 5, which is non-zero (true), causing the if branch to execute. Misconception: Believing it tests whether x equals 5.',1,'What is printed by the following C code?\n\n#include <stdio.h>\nint main() {\n    int x = 0;\n    if (x = 5) {\n        printf(\"True\");\n    } else {\n        printf(\"False\");\n    }\n    return 0;\n}','MCQ',NULL,4,20,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',0,1,NULL),(21,'2026-11-02 06:42:14.209583','MEDIUM','In C switch statements, execution enters at case 2 (\'Tue \') and falls through all subsequent cases because there are no break statements, printing \'Tue Wed End\'. Misconception: Forgetting that switch cases fall through automatically without explicit break.',1,'What is printed by this switch statement?\n\n#include <stdio.h>\nint main() {\n    int day = 2;\n    switch (day) {\n        case 1: printf(\"Mon \");\n        case 2: printf(\"Tue \");\n        case 3: printf(\"Wed \");\n        default: printf(\"End\");\n    }\n    return 0;\n}','MCQ',NULL,4,21,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',0,1,NULL),(22,'2026-11-02 06:42:14.212582','MEDIUM','In C syntax, an else clause always associates with the nearest unmatched preceding if statement. Despite the misleading indentation, the else belongs to if (b > 0). Since a > 0 is true and b > 0 is false, it prints \'Y\'. Misconception: Relying on visual indentation instead of compiler parsing rules.',1,'Consider the classic dangling-else problem:\n\n#include <stdio.h>\nint main() {\n    int a = 1, b = 0;\n    if (a > 0)\n        if (b > 0)\n            printf(\"X\");\n    else\n        printf(\"Y\");\n    return 0;\n}\n\nWhat does this print?','MCQ',NULL,4,22,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',0,1,NULL),(23,'2026-11-02 06:42:14.215583','HARD','Switch case labels in C must be compile-time integer constant expressions (such as char, int, or enum). Floating-point literals (like 2.5f) are explicitly prohibited. Misconception: Expecting switch statements to support floating-point values or arbitrary variables.',1,'Which of the following expressions CANNOT be used as a case label in a C switch statement?','MCQ',NULL,4,23,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',0,1,NULL),(24,'2026-11-02 06:42:14.217582','HARD','The semicolon immediately following if (x > 5) acts as an empty null statement. The following block { printf(\"Hello\"); } is an independent compound statement that always executes regardless of the condition. Misconception: Not realizing that an accidental semicolon decouples the block from the if check.',1,'What is printed by the following code?\n\n#include <stdio.h>\nint main() {\n    int x = 10;\n    if (x > 5)\n        ;\n    {\n        printf(\"Hello\");\n    }\n    return 0;\n}','MCQ',NULL,4,24,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',0,1,NULL),(25,'2026-11-02 06:42:14.219582','EASY','The loop variable i takes the sequential values 0, 1, 2, 3, and 4. When i reaches 5, the condition 5 < 5 is false, yielding exactly 5 iterations. Misconception: Off-by-one errors (thinking 0 to < 5 means 4 iterations).',1,'How many times does the body of this loop execute?\n\nint count = 0;\nfor (int i = 0; i < 5; i++) {\n    count++;\n}','MCQ',NULL,5,25,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',0,1,NULL),(26,'2026-11-02 06:42:14.222582','EASY','A while loop tests its condition before entering the body (entry-controlled), while a do-while loop evaluates its condition at the bottom (exit-controlled), guaranteeing at least one execution. Misconception: Assuming all loop types evaluate their condition before any statements execute.',1,'How is a do-while loop fundamentally different from a while loop in C?','MCQ',NULL,5,26,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',0,1,NULL),(27,'2026-11-02 06:42:14.224583','MEDIUM','When i reaches 3, the break statement terminates the while loop immediately before printing 3 or incrementing i. Therefore, only \'1 2 \' are printed. Misconception: Confusing break (exits entire loop) with continue (skips to next iteration).',1,'What is printed by this loop?\n\n#include <stdio.h>\nint main() {\n    int i = 1;\n    while (i <= 5) {\n        if (i == 3) {\n            break;\n        }\n        printf(\"%d \", i);\n        i++;\n    }\n    return 0;\n}','MCQ',NULL,5,27,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',0,1,NULL),(28,'2026-11-02 06:42:14.226583','MEDIUM','The loop iterates through i = 0, 1, 2, 3. When i is 2, continue skips sum += i. Thus sum = 0 + 1 + 3 = 4. Misconception: Adding all numbers (0+1+2+3=6) without accounting for the continue jump.',1,'What is the final value of sum in this snippet?\n\n#include <stdio.h>\nint main() {\n    int sum = 0;\n    for (int i = 0; i < 4; i++) {\n        if (i == 2) continue;\n        sum += i;\n    }\n    printf(\"%d\", sum);\n    return 0;\n}','MCQ',NULL,5,28,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',0,1,NULL),(29,'2026-11-02 06:42:14.228583','HARD','Iteration 1: prints \'1 \', x becomes 3; condition (3 < 5) is true. Iteration 2: prints \'3 \', x becomes 5; condition (5 < 5) is false. Loop finishes. Output is \'1 3 \'. Misconception: Expecting 5 to be printed before the condition check terminates the loop.',1,'What will be printed by the following do-while loop?\n\n#include <stdio.h>\nint main() {\n    int x = 1;\n    do {\n        printf(\"%d \", x);\n        x += 2;\n    } while (x < 5);\n    return 0;\n}','MCQ',NULL,5,29,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',0,1,NULL),(30,'2026-11-02 06:42:14.231584','HARD','The loop increments c from 0 through 254 (255 iterations). When c becomes 255, the condition (255 < 255) is false, so it terminates after 255 iterations. (Note: if the condition were c <= 255, it would be an infinite loop because 255 + 1 wraps to 0). Misconception: Generalizing that any loop with unsigned char wrapping is automatically infinite.',1,'Consider this loop:\n\n#include <stdio.h>\nint main() {\n    unsigned char c;\n    int iterations = 0;\n    for (c = 0; c < 255; c++) {\n        iterations++;\n    }\n    printf(\"%d\", iterations);\n    return 0;\n}\n\nWhat does this print?','MCQ',NULL,5,30,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',0,1,NULL),(31,'2026-11-02 06:42:14.233583','EASY','C is strictly a pass-by-value language. Arguments passed into functions are copied; modifications to local parameter copies do not alter the caller\'s variables unless explicit pointers are passed. Misconception: Believing C supports pass-by-reference natively without pointers.',1,'In C, how are arguments passed to functions by default?','MCQ',NULL,6,31,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',0,1,NULL),(32,'2026-11-02 06:42:14.235583','EASY','A function prototype declares the function\'s name, return type, and parameter list before its definition, enabling the compiler to validate argument types and counts at call sites. Misconception: Confusing a declaration with code generation or memory allocation.',1,'What is a function prototype in C and why is it used?','MCQ',NULL,6,32,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',0,1,NULL),(33,'2026-11-02 06:42:14.237583','MEDIUM','Because parameters are passed by value, swap operates on isolated local copies of x and y. The original variables in main remain unchanged as \'10 20\'. Misconception: Believing a swap function without pointer dereferencing can mutate the caller\'s values.',1,'What is printed by this program?\n\n#include <stdio.h>\nvoid swap(int a, int b) {\n    int temp = a;\n    a = b;\n    b = temp;\n}\nint main() {\n    int x = 10, y = 20;\n    swap(x, y);\n    printf(\"%d %d\", x, y);\n    return 0;\n}','MCQ',NULL,6,33,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',0,1,NULL),(34,'2026-11-02 06:42:14.239582','MEDIUM','In a void function, \'return;\' serves as an early exit command, returning control immediately to the caller without supplying a value. Misconception: Believing void functions cannot contain any return statements or must return 0.',1,'What does the return statement do in a function whose return type is void?','MCQ',NULL,6,34,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',0,1,NULL),(35,'2026-11-02 06:42:14.242583','HARD','A static local variable is initialized once and retains its value between successive function calls across the program\'s lifetime. Calling counter() 3 times increments count from 0 to 1, 2, and 3, printing \'1 2 3 \'. Misconception: Assuming local variables always re-initialize to 0 on every call.',1,'What is printed by the following code with a static local variable?\n\n#include <stdio.h>\nvoid counter() {\n    static int count = 0;\n    count++;\n    printf(\"%d \", count);\n}\nint main() {\n    counter();\n    counter();\n    counter();\n    return 0;\n}','MCQ',NULL,6,35,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',0,1,NULL),(36,'2026-11-02 06:42:14.248582','HARD','This computes factorial: 4 * mystery(3) = 4 * 3 * mystery(2) = 4 * 3 * 2 * 1 = 24. Misconception: Confusing factorial multiplication with recursive addition (which would yield 10).',1,'What is the output of this recursive function?\n\n#include <stdio.h>\nint mystery(int n) {\n    if (n <= 1) return 1;\n    return n * mystery(n - 1);\n}\nint main() {\n    printf(\"%d\", mystery(4));\n    return 0;\n}','MCQ',NULL,6,36,'MANUAL','ACTIVE',0,1,NULL);
